{
 "id": "2021-kim-busan",
 "name": "Busan Street Tree Preference and Perception (multi-rating, ranking, similarity)",
 "description": "Faithful replication of the street-tree survey of Kim, Lee, Lee & Choi (2021), Citizens' Preference and Perception of Street Trees of Main Boulevards in Busan, South Korea, Sustainability 13(6): 3141. Participants see a street photograph of each of the 12 most common street tree species in Busan (Yoshino cherry, maidenhair, sawleaf zelkova, bottonwood, silver magnolia, Chinese scholar, Retusa fringe, Japanese black pine, round-leaf holly, trident maple, Japanese horse chestnut, Japanese hackberry). They (1) rate each tree on 15 five-point word pairs: six visual properties (simple-complex, covered-exposed, closed-open, common-unique, low-high nuisance, unfamiliar-familiar) and nine affective properties (pleasant, exciting, relaxing, safe, interesting, beautiful, good, likable, warm); (2) name their three most and three least preferred species in order and say why; (3) rate how similar every pair of species looks (66 pairs, for MDS); then give gender, age, education, monthly income and years of residence. In the original, 128 Busan citizens took part; Yoshino cherry was named most preferred by 37.1% and maidenhair least preferred by 22.1% (embedded in the page for comparison). Photographs are the street-context pictures of the paper's Figure 2 (CC BY 4.0), used unmodified with credit to the authors; the paper's second picture per species, taken from a book, is not used. The template will be removed if the authors object. Differences from the original study: (1) a web survey instead of a face-to-face paper questionnaire with 7 x 8 cm printed pictures; (2) English species names instead of the Korean names used in the survey; (3) the paper does not print which end of each word pair was 1 or 5, so the negative/first word is 1 and the positive/second word is 5; (4) reasons for the most and least preferred tree are open text only, because the paper does not list the answer options; (5) trees and similarity pairs are shown in random order (left/right position of each pair also random); (6) residence asks about \"this city\" instead of Busan.",
